; 18-assume.kurt

; `assume` opens a block that hypothesizes a formula, to be discharged again
; once the block closes.  Closing it (here: by dedenting) triggers
; "impl-intro": if you can derive `Q` after assuming `P`, you get
; `P implies Q` back, outside the block.

prop A, B, C
use A implies B
use B implies C

show A implies C
proof
    assume A
        B
        C
qed

; `assume` doesn't need a `proof`: at the top level, the block closes the
; same way, and the line `A implies C` is a fact of the file from then on --
; without a `show` that promised it first.
assume A
    B
    C
    B and C
A implies (B and C)             ; Kurt: by 22-25, the lines of the block

; The same mechanism gives "not-intro": assume `P`, derive `false`, and you
; get `not P`.  `not` and `false`'s proof rules live in `propositional.kurt`
; (lesson 10):
load propositional

prop p, q
use p implies q
use not q

show not p
proof
    assume p
        q
        false
qed
